# 6. Attributed Pathway

An attributed pathway is the fundamental computational lineage object.

A pathway is represented as:

$$
\gamma=
(o_0,c_1,r_1,t_1,
c_2,r_2,t_2,
\ldots,
c_n,r_n,t_n)
$$

with each transition retaining:

* originating observation;
* capability identifier;
* capability version;
* intermediate representation;
* timestamp;
* provenance;
* input references;
* output references;
* admission state where applicable.

Therefore:

$$
\gamma \in \Gamma
$$

where \(\Gamma\) is the set of pathways satisfying Fabric transition rules.

A pathway MUST be reconstructable.

A pathway MUST NOT be represented solely as an opaque execution log if doing so prevents reconstruction of its computational lineage.

---

# 7. Pathway Measure

The persistent computational state of the Cognitive Fabric is:

$$
\nu_t\in\mathcal M_+(\Gamma)
$$

where \(\mathcal M_+(\Gamma)\) is the space of positive measures over admissible pathways.

The measure may encode, subject to implementation:

* pathway existence;
* pathway recurrence;
* pathway confidence;
* temporal persistence;
* computational activation;
* pathway relevance;
* provenance weight;
* cross-capability traversal;
* cross-domain traversal.

The concrete representation of \(\nu_t\) remains an implementation decision.

Possible realizations include:

* sparse indexed pathway stores;
* weighted temporal hypergraphs;
* event-sourced pathway state;
* vector-plus-lineage representations;
* graph/path measures;
* distributed state stores.

No representation is normative until experimentally validated.

The architectural requirement is that the representation preserve the semantics of:

$$
\nu_t
$$

rather than reducing the Fabric to endpoint embeddings alone.

