# 13. Formation Frontier

A Formation Frontier is an actionable observational boundary associated with an existing Formation.

Let:

$$
B(F,t)
$$

represent the observed boundary state of Formation \(F\).

A Frontier exists only where:

$$
FF(F,t)
=
S
\land
G
\land
R
\land
T
\land
P
$$

where:

* \(S\) = structural relevance;
* \(G\) = identified observational gap;
* \(R\) = evidence resolvability;
* \(T\) = targetability;
* \(P\) = provenance continuity.

Thus:

$$
FF(F,t)=1
$$

only when all governing Frontier conditions are satisfied.

---

# 14. Frontier Conditions

## FF1 — Existing Formation

The Frontier MUST originate from a qualifying Formation.

## FF2 — Observational Insufficiency

There MUST be an identifiable observation limitation.

## FF3 — Structural Relevance

The limitation MUST affect an existing structural question.

## FF4 — Potential Resolution

Additional observation MUST have a plausible ability to resolve the identified question.

## FF5 — Targetability

The system MUST be able to express the requested additional observation in executable terms.

## FF6 — Provenance

The Frontier MUST retain lineage to the Formation and evidence that produced it.

If any required condition fails:

$$
FF(F,t)=0.
$$

The system MUST NOT issue frontier-directed cognition.

