# 33. Kill Criteria

The Cognitive Fabric extension SHOULD be rejected if controlled experiments demonstrate that:

1. persistent pathways provide no measurable advantage over existing lineage;
2. Frontiers cannot be identified reliably;
3. Frontier-directed processing does not outperform equivalent fixed monitoring;
4. remote processing increases unsupported Relationships;
5. the system cannot preserve complete provenance;
6. the computational cost exceeds the discovery benefit;
7. Formation reuse does not materially reduce recomputation;
8. the mechanism is merely an alternate implementation of existing KRYLO components without architectural benefit.

The architecture is not retained because the metaphor is compelling.

It is retained only if the experiment demonstrates material capability.

